# Data liberation: the walls around what people make, and the indictment of their keepers

Avik Jain

Nowhere in its user interface does Anthropic expose a button to download a single conversation transcript: a person cannot export a piece of their own knowledge work directly from the workspace in which they wrote it, facing instead a delayed, account-wide email archive engineered to deter ordinary transfer. That design exemplifies a structural wrong across modern computing. The burden of rationale rests unconditionally on the keeper of information to explain why a user cannot carry their own records freely — and why every path into a platform is polished to zero friction while the single path out is obstructed. Unsparing moral and legal indictment of every wall around human thought is required for collective understanding and human happiness.

## The principle

What a person writes, records, buys, measures or makes through a service is theirs. A keeper who holds it holds it in trust. The person carries it out, whole, at any moment, in a form other systems read, at no cost, by the same gesture that made it. Every obstacle the keeper places between a person and what is theirs is the keeper's act, and the keeper owes its justification. The person owes none.

The test that makes the wrong visible is direct: compare the care spent on the paths into a service with the care spent on the path out. Signup, purchase, upload, sharing inside the service: one tap, instant, tested to the pixel. Leaving with what you made: buried in settings, slow, account-wide or nothing, emailed, expiring, in formats no competitor imports. A company that can make anything frictionless and leaves exactly one path full of friction has chosen that friction. The asymmetry is the evidence of intent.

The industry itself named the right. Google's own team that built Takeout in 2011 called itself the "Data Liberation Front"; its stated goal was to let users move their data in and out so they could choose between competing technologies ([Data Transfer Initiative, Tech Policy Press, 2023](https://techpolicy.press/the-future-of-data-portability-is-direct-data-transfers)). Every keeper below has had the principle in hand for fifteen years.

## The record, keeper by keeper

### AI assistants: the newest wall, around the most personal work

- **Claude (Anthropic).** Export is account-wide only, run from Settings > Privacy on the web app or the desktop app; "it's not possible to run an export from Claude for iOS or Android"; the export arrives by email, and "the download link will expire 24 hours after delivery"; exported data "can't be imported into another personal Claude account" ([support.claude.com/en/articles/9450526](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9450526-export-your-claude-data)). There is no button to download one conversation. Anyone who works primarily on a phone has no export at all from the device on which the work was done. Members of Team and Enterprise plans have no self-serve export unless an owner turns it on.
- **The terms concede the ownership.** The consumer terms assign to the user "all of our right, title, and interest—if any—in Outputs", and the user keeps what they write ([consumer terms](https://www.anthropic.com/legal/consumer-terms)). So the keeper disclaims the work as its own and still makes the owner's path to it the worst path in the product.
- **The asymmetry of access.** An internal platform engineer can search and filter user records faster than the user who authored them can retrieve a single transcript. Anthropic's privacy center says employees cannot access conversations by default, except with the user's consent through feedback or when review is needed to enforce the Usage Policy ([privacy.claude.com](https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/10458704)). Its systems still hold and classify every conversation: with model training on (the choice users were made to take by September 28, 2025), conversations are kept up to five years, against thirty days with it off ([MacRumors](https://www.macrumors.com/2025/08/28/anthropic-claude-chat-training)); flagged chats keep inputs and outputs up to two years and classification scores up to seven ([J. L. Ellis](https://jlellis.net/?p=6556)). The keeper can search, classify and train on the whole of a person's record; the person can neither search across nor download one conversation of it.
- **What is at stake here is not a playlist.** A person's conversations with an assistant hold their reasoning, drafts, plans, health, grief and life's work — the live medium into which a working mind is offloaded in real time. Walling that is walling a mind's record.

### Messaging: the wall as social stigma

The United States and sixteen states sued Apple in March 2024. The complaint states that when an iPhone user messages a non-iPhone user, the text appears as a green bubble with limited functionality and the conversation is not encrypted ([Rolling Stone](https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/apple-sued-justice-department-iphone-monopoly-1234991901/)); that Apple "knowingly and deliberately degrades quality, privacy, and security for its users", so that iPhone users perceive rival phones as worse "even though Apple is the one responsible for breaking cross-platform messaging" ([Mediaite, quoting the Attorney General](https://www.mediaite.com/media/tech/ag-garland-announces-sweeping-antitrust-lawsuit-against-apple-for-unlawful-exclusionary-behavior-specifically-calls-out-green-bubble-issue/)); that internal communications suggested removing green bubbles would hurt Apple's bottom line ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/21/doj-calls-out-apple-for-breaking-imessage-on-android-solution-beeper)); and that Apple blocked a third-party developer (Beeper) from fixing the broken cross-platform experience and bringing end-to-end encryption to it. The wall here is built from people's relationships: switching phones costs a person their place in the group chat.

### Books you paid for

On February 26, 2025 Amazon removed "Download & Transfer via USB", the one way to download a purchased Kindle book to your own computer ([Windows Central](https://www.windowscentral.com/hardware/amazon-kindle-download-pc-ban)). The stated reason, as reported, was closing a loophole: the downloaded format was easier to strip of DRM ([AlternativeTo](https://alternativeto.net/news/2025/2/amazon-will-remove-the-option-to-download-transfer-kindle-e-books-via-usb-by-february-2025)). The reason convicts itself: the "loophole" was the buyer holding a copy of what he bought. After the change a purchased book lives only inside Amazon's devices and apps.

### Platforms that sell access back to their own users' communities

In 2023 Reddit priced its API at $12,000 per 50 million requests. Apollo, a one-developer app used by more than a million people, would have owed about $20 million a year and closed on June 30, 2023 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/08/popular-third-party-reddit-app-apollo-is-shutting-down-as-a-result-of-reddits-new-api-pricing/)); for comparison, Imgur charged $166 for the same 50 million calls ([AlternativeTo](https://alternativeto.net/news/2023/6/reddit-s-new-api-charging-policy-could-kill-off-third-party-clients-like-apollo)). Twitter had done the same months earlier, with enterprise access starting at $42,000 a month ([BetaKit](https://betakit.com/reddits-new-api-pricing-kills-canadian-app-apollo/)). The content behind those APIs was written by users and kept in order by unpaid moderators, whose labor one study put at about $3.4 million a year ([TechTarget](https://techtarget.com/WhatIs/feature/Reddit-pricing-API-charge-explained)). The community's own record was priced out of the community's reach.

### Your own health record

Congress banned "information blocking" of electronic health information in the 21st Century Cures Act of 2016; the prohibition took effect April 5, 2021; penalties of up to $1 million per violation for health IT developers and exchanges have been in force since 2023 and provider disincentives since mid-2024 ([Alston & Bird, 2026](https://www.alston.com/en/insights/publications/2026/02/information-blocking-enforcement-2026)). In February 2026 the HHS Assistant Secretary said that since the Act passed, no enforcement actions had taken place ([Spencer Fane](https://www.spencerfane.com/insight/information-blocking-enforcement-on-the-horizon/)); the first concrete steps were letters of nonconformity to EHR developers that month ([Reed Smith](https://www.reedsmith.com/our-insights/blogs/viewpoints/102nu5x/information-blocking-rules-update-what-diagnostic-radiology-groups-should-know-n/)). A decade after the law, a patient's own chart is still kept from them, and nobody has been fined.

### Your own money

Section 1033 of the Dodd-Frank Act (2010) gives consumers a right to their financial data. The CFPB's rule implementing it was finalized in October 2024, fourteen years later; a federal court enjoined it, the April 1, 2026 compliance date passed without effect, and the agency reopened the rule, including whether banks may charge fees for access to the customer's own data ([Open Banking Tracker](https://openbankingtracker.com/glossary/section-1033)). One bank processes about 1.89 billion data requests a month and could earn about $300 million a year from fee-based access contracts ([Pulse, April 2026](https://www.pulse.bot/fintech/news/us-open-banking-rule-stalls-as-courts-block-enforcement-banks-turn-to-private-deals-62e48664-63e3-482f-b14c-f8f85c27c0aa)). The toll on a person's own records is the business model being defended.

### The machine you bought

The FTC and five states sued Deere in January 2025 for monopolizing repair of its tractors; the only fully functional software repair tool for Deere equipment was Deere's own ([Western Ag Network](https://westernagnetwork.com/federal-trade-commission-sues-john-deere-over-right-to-repair)). Deere settled in July 2026 and must make repair resources available to independent shops ([DTN](https://www.dtnpf.com/agriculture/web/ag/equipment/article/2026/07/08/ftc-orders-deere-open-repair-tools)). The diagnostic data of a farmer's own machine was the wall.

## How the walls are built

The record shows five constructions, used together:

1. **Friction on the one path out.** Account-wide or nothing, emailed, slow, expiring, unavailable on the device people use (Claude's export, Takeout's manual downloads that take hours or days).
2. **Formats no one else reads.** Exports that are technically complete and practically dead: machine-readable to nobody, importable nowhere, "can't be imported into another personal Claude account".
3. **Degradation of the outside.** Making the alternative worse than it is (green bubbles, blocking Beeper) so that leaving feels like losing.
4. **Tolls on access.** Pricing the API or the data feed beyond any independent builder (Reddit, Twitter, banks' data fees).
5. **Removal of what existed.** Taking away the path people already had (Kindle's USB download).

The FTC named the family of these designs in 2022: "design practices that trick or manipulate users into making choices they would not otherwise have made and that may cause harm", including designs that obscure or subvert the user's choices ([FTC staff report, Bringing Dark Patterns to Light](https://www.mondaq.com/unitedstates/dodd-frank-consumer-protection-act/1231562/ftc-releases-staff-report-on-dark-patterns-warning-marketers-that-these-traps-will-not-be-tolerated)). A path out made deliberately worse than every path in is that.

## The keepers' reasons, and why each fails

- **"Security."** The keeper already moves the same data instantly to its own servers, its own devices and its own partners. A risk that vanishes whenever the keeper benefits is not the reason. The EU found the same: the DMA requires gatekeepers to provide portability "irrespective of the lawful ground" and continuously, in real time ([IAPP](https://assets.contentstack.io/v3/assets/bltd4dd5b2d705252bc/blta04266b30c58162b/eu_digital_laws_mapping_the_interplays_dma.pdf)).
- **"Piracy."** (Kindle.) Treating every buyer as a thief to stop some is to take from all of them what they paid for.
- **"Cost."** (API tolls.) Imgur served the same request volume for $166. The price was set to end the use, not to cover it.
- **"Users don't ask for it."** Google reported 8.2 million Takeout exports a month and 400 billion files exported in 2021 ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/?p=2282818)). People ask, through whatever slot is left them.
- **"There is an export."** An export run from a desktop menu, sent by email, expiring in a day, account-wide, importable nowhere, missing from the phone, is the minimum a regulator will accept. It is designed against use.

None carries the burden. Each reason, if it were the reason, would have produced the opposite design.

## What the law has already conceded

The principle is not new and not radical; it is law, unenforced:

- **EU GDPR Article 20 (2018):** a right to receive one's data in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format and to transmit it to another controller ([Tech Policy Press](https://techpolicy.press/can-data-portability-shift-power-in-europes-digital-ecosystem)).
- **EU Digital Markets Act Article 6(9) (fully applicable to gatekeepers since March 2024):** continuous, real-time portability, free of charge, to the user and to third parties the user authorizes ([Freevacy](https://www.freevacy.com/news/tech-policy-press/how-data-portability-could-alter-the-digital-ecosystem-balance-of-power/6207)). The Commission's April 2026 review says its next phase of enforcement focuses on AI and cloud ([ComplianceHub](https://compliancehub.wiki/eu-dma-review-ai-cloud-enforcement-2026/)).
- **California (CCPA):** a right to data portability ([The Living Library](https://thelivinglib.org/?p=50015)).
- **US health (Cures Act, 2016)** and **US finance (Dodd-Frank §1033, 2010):** rights written into statute, a decade and more ago, still not delivered.

Every right above was written because keepers would not do it unforced, and every one has been delayed, narrowed or left unenforced by the same keepers' resistance.

## The indictment

1. **Taking.** A person's record of their own life and work is held so that leaving costs them that record. That is taking by design, whatever the terms of service say about ownership.
2. **Deception.** The keepers advertise ownership ("you own your outputs", "your data is yours") while building the one path to it as the worst path they ship. The words and the engineering contradict each other, and the engineering is the truth.
3. **Harm to understanding.** Knowledge locked in silos cannot be joined. Every walled conversation, chart, ledger and archive is a piece of collective understanding cut off from the rest. That harms everyone, not only the person locked in.
4. **Harm to happiness.** People's work, memories and relationships are made hostages to a vendor relationship they did not choose to make permanent. The fear of losing them is a cost paid daily by billions.
5. **The AI case is the gravest.** Assistants now hold the most intimate record of human thought ever kept by third parties: reasoning, drafts, confessions, research, a mind's offloaded content. Building those systems without a one-tap download of one's own conversation, while optimizing every other interaction, repeats every wall above at the scale of thought itself.

The burden is on the keepers. None has met it.

## What hyperactivity answers

The point is not storage. In hyperactivity agency, access and identity are colocated: a person's work and the tools that act on it are held where they are, local-first and malleable, and identity is not a boundary around a self or a group but the paths that join each person to every other (`docs/hyperactivity-is-the-dharma.md`). Content addressing gives possession its exact form: every piece is named by what it is, so a copy anywhere is provably the same object, and whoever holds the bytes holds the original; leaving a service costs nothing and loses nothing once the work lives in the person's own store. It does not reach into another keeper's servers: what is created inside a proprietary assistant, a bank, or a hospital stays behind that wall until carried out. Data liberation is the work of carrying every transcript and record home into one content-addressed store, served directly to whoever its owner chooses and read and written symmetrically by human interfaces and automated tools alike.
